  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nA cold front extending from the Kankakee River Valley in \nnorthwest Indiana to just northwest of Champaign will continue \nto generate a broken line of thunderstorms over the next hour or\nso before exiting to the east. Brief instances of large hail \nand damaging wind remain possible. Farther northwest, low-level \nmixing and some mid-level warming is gradually reducing MLCAPE \nbehind the front. While isolated showers remain possible as a \nweak trailing mid-level wave drags across northern Illinois this\nafternoon, latest trends favor primarily dry conditions.\n\nHigh pressure will build across the western Great Lakes on \nThursday before drifting eastward Friday and Saturday. Precip-\nfree conditions are expected during this time with the exception\nof isolated showers or sprinkles on a lake breeze boundary \nacross northeast Illinois Thursday afternoon.\n\nA loosely zonal pattern transitioning to low-amplitude ridging \nacross North America this weekend into the middle of next week \nfavors a more active storm track north of the forecast area. WAA\nand an embedded mid-level wave on the leading edge of the ridge\nSaturday night into Sunday morning may result in convection \nextending as far south as northern Illinois. Otherwise, summer \nwarmth will return with increasing humidity and daytime highs \npushing well into the 80s, and possibly low 90s, if the active \nstorm track does remain north of the area.\n\nKluber",
